# Threat model — {{ display_name }}

## Hallucination
The optional LLM may propose signals, bounds, or properties that do not exist.
Mitigation: every identifier is grounded against a supplied symbol table; ungrounded
identifiers are rejected by `validators.py`, never emitted.

## Unsafe assumptions
An LLM might turn a design guarantee into an environment assumption. Mitigation:
property kind (assume/assert/cover) is explicit in the typed IR and changes require
human review (see manifest `required_human_approvals`).

## Data leakage
Only public, non-proprietary inputs are permitted. `audit-public-release` in the
factory scans for secrets and proprietary markers before release.

## Reproducibility risks
Non-deterministic LLM output would break reproducibility. Mitigation: the default
adapter is the deterministic mock; real providers are opt-in and their versions
are recorded in provenance.

## Result-classification risks
A timeout/error/unknown must never be reported as PASS. The result vocabulary is
enforced: PASS, FAIL, TIMEOUT, ERROR, UNKNOWN, "property compiled" (syntax only).
